22:53:08 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: When you exhale with your mouth wide open, the air leaves slowly and feels warm because it’s been heated by your body. But when you purse your lips and blow a narrow stream, the same air feels cool—even though it’s still the same temperature when it leaves your lungs. The change in perceived temperature comes down to a combination of fluid dynamics and thermodynamics: **1. Speed and pressure change (Bernoulli’s principle)** By narrowing the opening with pursed lips, you force the same volume of air through a smaller area. This causes the air to **accelerate**. According to Bernoulli’s principle, as the speed of a fluid (or gas) increases, its pressure decreases. **2. Adiabatic cooling** As the high-speed jet of air exits your mouth, the sudden drop in pressure allows the air to **expand rapidly**. This expansion happens without exchanging heat with the surroundings (adiabatic process), and expansion of a gas causes it to **cool down** significantly. That’s why the stream feels cool on your skin. **3. Entrainment of ambient air** The fast-moving jet also drags in and mixes with the cooler, still air around it (entrainment). This amplifies the cooling effect, making the air hitting your skin even cooler than the already-expanded breath. **In contrast, with a wide-open mouth:** Air leaves slowly, so there’s almost no pressure drop, negligible acceleration, little to no adiabatic expansion, and minimal mixing. The air reaching your skin is still at roughly body temperature—hence it feels warm. So the “temperature” your skin senses isn’t just the initial temperature of the air—it’s the result of **pressure, expansion, and mixing** triggered by the shape of your mouth.
